    "clinical": "<b>Practical Smear Examination for Blood Parasites.</b><br>When examining a Leishman-stained blood smear for haemoprotozoan diseases in Indian cattle: always examine the <b>feathered edge</b> under oil immersion. *Babesia bigemina* (large paired pyriform trophozoites joined at acute angle inside RBCs) and *Theileria annulata* (small signet-ring, comma, or rod-shaped piroplasms inside RBCs) accumulate heavily at the feathered edge because parasitised, damaged erythrocytes are lighter and carry to the smear periphery.",
